---
title: "What is Lesli? — A Modular Ruby on Rails SaaS Framework"
description: "Lesli is an open-source, modular Ruby on Rails framework for building SaaS products and long-lived business software."
---

<header class="about-header">
    <%= partial("partials/lesli-navigation", locals: { logo: true }) %>

    <div class="container about-hero">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">Lesli in one minute</p>
        <h1>A modular Rails foundation for <span class="lesli-title-colored">serious business software.</span></h1>
        <p class="about-introduction">
            Lesli gives Ruby on Rails developers a production-ready foundation for SaaS products,
            so they can build the business logic that makes their product unique—not the same infrastructure again.
        </p>

        <ul class="about-pillars" aria-label="What Lesli provides">
            <li class="about-pillar about-pillar--rails">
                <span class="about-pillar-icon ri-code-s-slash-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <strong>Rails-first</strong>
                <span>Rails, PostgreSQL, Hotwire, and familiar conventions.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="about-pillar about-pillar--saas">
                <span class="about-pillar-icon ri-stack-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <strong>SaaS-ready</strong>
                <span>Accounts, roles, admin, audit, notifications, and more.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="about-pillar about-pillar--modular">
                <span class="about-pillar-icon ri-box-3-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <strong>Modular</strong>
                <span>Add engines as your product grows and keep domains isolated.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>

    </div>
</header>

<main class="about-content">
    <section class="about-explanation">
        <div class="container">
            <p class="about-section-label">How it works</p>
            <h2>Start with structure. Keep Rails freedom.</h2>
            <p class="about-explanation-copy">
                Lesli runs inside a regular Rails application. The core defines shared conventions for accounts,
                users, navigation, assets, and engine integration. Official engines add business capabilities
                without folding every domain into the host application. You keep Rails as the development model
                and decide which parts of the ecosystem your product needs.
            </p>

            <figure class="about-diagram">
                <img
                    src="/images/engines/lesli/structure.excalidraw.svg"
                    alt="Three-step diagram showing a Rails application using Lesli Core and optional modules to become an owned SaaS product"
                    loading="lazy"
                />
                <figcaption>
                    Your Rails application remains the host. Lesli supplies the shared foundation; you choose the
                    modules and keep ownership of the workflows, integrations, brand, and data.
                </figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-use">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="about-use-copy">
                <p class="about-section-label">How to use it</p>
                <h2>From a Rails app to a working Lesli project.</h2>
                <p>
                    Install the core in a regular Rails application, prepare its database, and then add only the
                    engines your product needs. Your application stays a Rails application throughout the process.
                </p>

                <ol class="about-steps">
                    <li><strong>Install the core</strong> to add Lesli's shared structure and generators.</li>
                    <li><strong>Choose engines</strong> for capabilities such as access control, notifications, and auditing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Build your domain</strong> in the host app or in your own isolated Rails engine.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <div class="about-code" aria-label="Lesli quick start commands">
                <div class="about-code-heading">
                    <span>Quick start</span>
                    <span>Terminal</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code>rails new MySaaS
cd MySaaS
bundle add lesli
bin/rails generate lesli:install
bin/rails lesli:db:prepare
bin/rails server</code></pre>
                <a href="/engines/lesli/start/">
                    Read the installation guide
                    <span class="ri-arrow-right-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-fit">
        <div class="container">
            <p class="about-section-label">When Lesli fits</p>
            <h2>Built for software expected to grow.</h2>
            <p>
                Lesli is most useful when a product will outgrow a simple CRUD application: multi-account SaaS,
                internal operations platforms, or business systems expected to evolve for years. It is not a
                no-code builder or a finished admin panel; it is an open-source foundation for developers who
                want to build deliberately.
            </p>

            <div class="about-actions">
                <a class="button-primary" href="/engines/lesli/start/">
                    Start building
                    <span class="ri-arrow-right-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </a>
                <a class="button-primary-ghost" href="/engines/lesli/about/architecture/">
                    Explore the architecture
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</main>
